    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a bit of a heavy conclusion. Some argue that the collapse was inevitable because of the underlying tribal divisions and that the intervention just sped up a process that was already happening. Why do you lean towards calling it a \"strategic failure\" rather than just an inevitable outcome of a failed state?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The argument that the collapse was inevitable is strong, as pre- and post-intervention political fragmentation was always a risk in the deeply divided state of the country.\n\nI lean towards \"strategic failure\" because the primary political and legal objective of the intervention was to promote a stable, democratic government and prevent a massive power vacuum, which it fundamentally failed to achieve.\n\nThe intervention, in the opinion of many realpolitik analysts, should have a clear \"end state,\" and the extreme, unmanaged level of state failure that followed indicates the political objective was not secured, regardless of the pre-existing tribal potential for instability.",
